While looking up something else on the gopher server here the other day, I
found the following in the University of Chicago menu, which I find disquieting
if construed literally:

>                          The University of Chicago
>                  Responsible Use of Academic Computing Resources
>                          Revision Date 21 June, 1993

[...]

>o Identify yourself clearly and accurately in electronic
>  communications.  Anonymous or pseudononymous written communications appear
>  to dissociate you from responsibility for your actions and are almost
>  always inappropriate.  Concealing or misrepresenting your name or
>  affiliation to mask irresponsible or offensive behavior is a serious abuse.
>  Using identifiers of other individuals as your own constitutes fraud.

It is uncertain to me, on the basis of language elsewhere in this otherwise
unexceptionable policy statement, whether the actions described above are
considered actionable _per se_.  It could well be that in practice, the policy
is invoked only in cases of genuine nuisance that are brought to their atten-
tion.  The potential for miscarriages of justice remains, IMHO, given the
breadth of the nominal policy.  Would listing something other than your legal
name in the handle field, or not putting anything there, be a technical vio-
lation?  Would substituting something original for the default "organization"
field in a usenet post?  Would using a Wizvax number to post to ASB?

I'm sure that their actual enforcement behavior is eminently motive-depen-
dent, but where are the guarantees that such common sense will prevail, partic-
ularly in view of the potential for subjectivity in defining "irresponsible or
offensive behavior?"  I may well be worrying about nothing, but this seems to
me like an issue potentially worth raising, in CAF or elsewhere.  (For a more
reasonable attitude on anonymity and handles, I suggest looking at the FAQ in
the soc.feminism newsgroup.)

                                                   Mitch Pravatiner
